---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";
---
<Base title="Street-view country classification · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="An 18-country CNN classification course project: model training, controlled experiments, validation analysis, and individual contribution."
  toc={true}>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/research#projects">← Course projects</a></p>
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">{projects.streetView.status}</p>
    <h1>{projects.streetView.title}</h1>
    <p class="!text-lg">{projects.streetView.question}</p>
    <p>A three-person deep-learning course project using street images from 18 countries.
      We trained a VGG-style convolutional network from random initialization and compared
      how data preparation, regularisation, and the training schedule affected validation performance.</p>

    <h2>My contribution</h2>
    <p>I trained the models, ran the experiments, and analysed performance metrics and errors.
      A teammate led the implementation of the core training pipeline; another teammate
      compiled the results and wrote the group report.</p>

    <h2>Experiments</h2>
    <p>The project recorded 17 training runs, varying image resolution, augmentation,
      dropout, mixup, the optimiser, and the training schedule. The central question was
      which choices helped under a comparable evaluation setup, and which apparent gains
      depended on how long the baseline had been trained.</p>
    <p>The final model had approximately 1.69 million parameters. Its best recorded accuracy
      on the held-out validation split was 82.31%, compared with 61.9% in the initial setup.
      This is a validation result, not a score on the private test set.</p>

    <h2>What the comparisons showed</h2>
    <p>Training choices interacted. A dropout setting that performed poorly after 80 epochs
      performed best in a longer run, and extending training from 200 to 250 epochs
      reduced validation accuracy. Confusion matrices helped identify which country
      distinctions remained difficult.</p>

    <h2>Methods and scope</h2>
    <p>PyTorch, convolutional networks, GPU training, image augmentation, checkpoint selection,
      controlled configuration comparisons, and error analysis. This was a completed team
      course project; the contribution described here is model training and experimental analysis.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
